Important scope consideration
Since your agreed assessment scope is limited to encrypted EML upload and decryption, this finding should be included in the formal report only if server-level error handling is considered part of the in-scope application.

Also, the screenshot shows an authenticated Windows user. Therefore, the proposed CVSS vector assumes the same disclosure can be reproduced without authentication. If Windows authentication is required, PR:N is not justified and the score must be recalculated.

For an authenticated-only disclosure with otherwise equivalent assumptions, the vector would be CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N — 3.1 (Low).










Vulnerability: Unencrypted HTTP Communication (Lack of HTTPS Enforcement)
Suggested severity: Medium
Affected application: MIP Decryption Service – UAT
Affected endpoint: http://mip-decryptionservice.webfarm-uat.ms.com/
Protocol: HTTP
CWE: CWE-319 – Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information
OWASP Top 10: A02:2021 – Cryptographic Failures
Vulnerability description
The MIP Decryption Service application is accessible over unencrypted HTTP instead of enforcing HTTPS communication. During testing, the application accepted an HTTP request and returned an HTTP response without redirecting the request to HTTPS.

The HTTP connection does not provide transport encryption, allowing information transmitted between the client and server to potentially be intercepted or modified by an attacker positioned on the network.
Impact
An attacker with the ability to monitor network traffic may capture sensitive information transmitted between the user's browser and the application, including session cookies, authentication information, and uploaded encrypted EML files.

An attacker may also modify requests or responses in transit, potentially affecting the integrity and confidentiality of the file upload and decryption workflow.
Mitigation
- Enforce HTTPS across the entire application and redirect all HTTP requests to HTTPS.
- Configure TLS 1.2 or TLS 1.3 with strong cipher suites.
- Enable the HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) response header.
- Set session cookies with the Secure, HttpOnly, and appropriate SameSite attributes.
- Ensure sensitive file uploads and decrypted file downloads are permitted only over HTTPS.
CVSS v3.1
Suggested score: 6.5 – Medium
CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N


This score assumes an attacker can intercept traffic and that sensitive session or application data is transmitted. Reassess the score based on the actual data exposed and attack prerequisites.
Proof of Concept (PoC)
1. Open Burp Suite and configure the browser to route application traffic through the Burp proxy.
2. Access the application using the HTTP URL:
   GET /bin HTTP/1.1
   Host: mip-decryptionservice.webfarm-uat.ms.com
3. Observe the response:
   HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found
   Server: Microsoft-IIS
   Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8
4. Verify that the server responds over HTTP instead of redirecting the request to HTTPS.
5. Check whether the application also permits authenticated upload/decryption requests and transmits session cookies over HTTP.
